From: Lei Wei <quic_leiwei@quicinc.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 13:34:14 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] net: pcs: ipq9574: implement link modes and UNIPHY bring-up

The 'UNIPHY' PCS block in the IPQ9574 SoC includes PCS and SerDes
functions. It supports different interface modes to enable Ethernet MAC
connections to different types of external PHYs/switch. It includes PCS
functions for 1Gbps and 2.5Gbps interface modes and XPCS functions for
10Gbps interface modes. There are three UNIPHY (PCS) instances in IPQ9574
SoC which provide PCS/XPCS functions to the six Ethernet ports.

The 'UNIPHY' PCS hardware block in Qualcomm's IPQ SoC supports different
interface modes to enable Ethernet MAC connections for different types of
external PHYs/switch. Each UNIPHY block includes a SerDes and PCS/XPCS
blocks, and can operate in either PCS or XPCS modes. It supports 1Gbps and
2.5Gbps interface modes (Ex: SGMII) using the PCS, and 10Gbps interface
modes (Ex: USXGMII) using the XPCS. There are three UNIPHY (PCS) instances
in IPQ9574 SoC which support the six Ethernet ports in the SoC.

This patch adds the following PCS functionality for the PCS driver for
IPQ9574 SoC:

USXGMII mode is enabled by PCS when 10Gbps PHYs are connected, such as
Aquantia 10Gbps PHY.

10GBASER mode is used when PCS connects with a 10G SFP module.

2500BASEX mode is used when PCS connects with QCA8386 switch in a fixed
2500M link. It is also used when PCS connectes with QCA8081 PHY which works
at 2500M link speed. In addition, it can be also used when PCS connects
with a 2.5G SFP module.

1000BASEX is used when PCS connects with a 1G SFP module.

10G_QXGMII is used when PCS connectes with QCA8084 four ports 2.5G PHYs.

When 2500base-x mode is enabled MISC2 regsister needs to have different
value than for other 1G modes.

Keep the PCS driver synced with the latest version posted to the kernel
community and add the XPCS reset support.

The XPCS signals USXGMII in-band autonegotiation completion through an
interrupt that is never enabled, waited for or acknowledged, so rate
adaptation never starts and the MAC receives nothing even though the SerDes
achieves block lock. The advertised speed and duplex are also left at their
reset values while autonegotiation runs.

Some board designs feed an external PHY its reference clock from the uniphy
25MHz clock output instead of a local crystal. Enable the output when the
devicetree opts in with "qcom,uniphy-clkout-25mhz".

The uniphy never receives a software reset pulse during interface mode
configuration. The vendor SSDK asserts the GCC system reset and the NSSCC
soft reset together for 100ms on every uniphy mode change; without the
pulse, parts of the uniphy analog block can remain in their power-on state.

The MISC2 phy mode field is modelled as two bits at the wrong offset: the
hardware field is three bits wide and starts one bit lower. The SGMII and
2500BASE-X values only come out right because the power-on default happens
to keep the missed bit set, and USXGMII is not programmed at all, leaving
the uniphy in its power-on SGMII personality.

Align the USXGMII configuration sequence with the SSDK:

Reuse the qcom-ipq9574 PCS driver for IPQ5332 with an explicit MII clock
rate request during link-up.

Use the SFP state machine to gate forced 1000BASE-X and 2500BASE-X links
without in-band negotiation. Select the SGMII+ MAC personality explicitly
without inheriting bootloader or previous interface settings.

